 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Generally slower than hardware-accelerated methods due to software scan conversion and sorting overhead [S1]. Performance depends on the number of triangles and the coherence between scan-lines [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the triangle list, y-bucket lists, and frame buffer. The size of the transformed vertex locations can be significant (e.g., 11.3 Mbytes for a space shuttle grid) [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** An average frame rate of 0.8 fps was reported with an image resolution of 512 × 512 pixels on a single PIV 1.9 GHz processor [S3]. Interactive rendering of mid-sized models is possible with 2 to 5 fps on more powerful hardware (e.g., SGI Onyx with four processors) [S3].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces image quality equivalent to ray casting with fewer artifacts than hardware Gouraud shading methods [S1]. Handles irregular and multiple grids effectively [S1]. Can be parallelized (e.g., distributing cells round-robin-like among nodes) [S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Slower than hardware-accelerated variants [S1]. Not optimized for opaque datasets where ray casting can halt early [S1]. Requires careful handling of depth sorting to avoid artifacts [S2, unofficial][S3].
-*   **When to Use:** When high-quality rendering of irregular/unstructured grids is required and hardware acceleration is not available or insufficient [S1]. When software-based control over compositing is needed [S7, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** For real-time rendering of large regular grids where hardware acceleration is preferred [S1]. For opaque datasets where ray casting can provide early termination [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Generally faster than ray casting due to the use of coherence between adjacent pixels and scan lines [S1]. However, it is slower than hardware-accelerated methods like Gouraud shading [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the Y-bucket lists and the image buffer. The size depends on the number of triangles and image resolution.
+*   **Measured Performance:** An average frame rate of 0.8 fps was reported with an image resolution of 512 × 512 pixels on a single PIV 1.9 GHz processor [S3]. Interactive rendering of mid-sized models is possible with 2 to 5 fps [S3]. Rendering images with a 512² viewport can take up to one minute or more on an SGI Onyx with four processors [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Produces image quality equivalent to ray casting but faster due to coherence [S1]. Yields noticeably clearer images with fewer artifacts than hardware Gouraud shading methods [S1]. Can handle irregular and multiple grids [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Slower than hardware methods [S1]. Ray casting can be competitive or faster on opaque datasets (e.g., head) because it can halt early when a pixel becomes opaque [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When high image quality is required for irregular/unstructured grids and hardware acceleration is not available or insufficient [S1][S7, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** For real-time rendering on opaque datasets where early ray termination in ray casting is effective [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method originated as an early software implementation based on projection, proposed by Lucas [Luc92], who sorted projected faces using the Painter’s Algorithm [S3]. Wilhelms et al. [WGTG96] improved this by using software scan conversion with y- and x-bucket lists to leverage coherence between adjacent pixels and scan-lines [S2, unofficial][S3]. Williams et al. [WMS98] developed an elaborate system based on projection, analytically solving the rendering equation for high-quality images [S3]. The method is also associated with Upson and Keeler [UK88], who presented a ray casting method and a projection method using transfer functions [S5, unofficial]. It has been extended to handle curvilinear grids [S3][S7, unofficial] and parallelized for distributed rendering [S3].
+The method was proposed as an early software implementation based on projection, where projected faces of an irregular grid are sorted using the Painter’s Algorithm [S3]. Lucas [Luc92] was one of the first to propose this, though his use of face centroids for sorting could lead to errors [S3][S2, unofficial]. Wilhelm et al. [WGTG96] improved this by using a software scan conversion that optimizes sorting by leveraging coherence between adjacent pixels and scan lines, using Y- and X-bucket lists [S2, unofficial][S3]. Williams et al. [WMS98] developed an elaborate volume rendering system purely based on projection, including detailed analytical solutions for the rendering equation [S3]. The method is also known as the Cell-by-Cell Method or Upson-Keller Method, referencing early work in ray casting and projection [S5, unofficial]. It is designed to run in parallel on MIMD architectures and handle multiple curvilinear grids [S7, unofficial].
 
